WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.300
- Thanks to CuriosityStream
for keeping LegalEagle and bees

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.980
everywhere in the air.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.490
- He's denouncing bees.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.403
- And don't y'all date your cousins?

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.330
- Objection.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.463
- Ah, the non-lawyer makes an objection.

00:00:13.463 --> 00:00:16.130
(playful music)

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.700
Hey, LegalEagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.870
Today we are covering the
highly requested Bee Movie,

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:27.280
an animated movie about
bees that sue humanity

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:28.620
for stealing their honey.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.410
I think this is the movie
I was born to review.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.440
Be sure to comment in
the form of an objection,

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.380
which I will either sustain or overrule,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.240
and stick around until
the end of this video

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.380
where I will give the Bee Movie
a grade for legal realism.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.513
So, without further ado,
let's dig in to the Bee Movie.

00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:47.840
(dramatic news music)

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.130
- Good evening, I'm Bob Bumble.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.490
- And I'm Jeanette Chung.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.180
- Our top story, a
tri-county bee, Barry Benson,

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.290
is saying he intends to sue the human race

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.930
for stealing our honey, packaging it,

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.220
and profiting from it illegally.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.910
- All right, so, right out of the gate,

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.410
big problem with Barry's lawsuit:

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.770
you cannot sue the entire human race.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.450
To sue a party in court,

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.650
they have to be a recognizable
entity that can be sued,

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.930
an individual, a corporation.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:13.920
Who would show up?

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.610
Who would hire the attorneys?

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.310
Who would be the material
witnesses in this case?

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.150
All of those are reasons why
the human race is not a party

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.840
that has standing to be sued in court.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.404
But it's similar to when
people have tried to sue God

00:01:27.404 --> 00:01:29.670
in federal and state court.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.420
Every few years you
get one of these jokers

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.000
who thinks it would be cute
to name God as a party,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.320
and every time it is rejected.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.940
Sometimes the courts just
dismiss the case out of hand

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.700
because there's no way
to properly notify God

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.440
because God doesn't have a fixed address

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.860
to accept service of the lawsuit,

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.860
and other times they will
let the lawsuit go forward

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.510
and let the case go into
what's called default,

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:56.210
which means that the
propounding party wins

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.890
because the other party didn't respond.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.760
But the problem is
there's no way to recover

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.940
whatever winnings you might
be entitled to from God,

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.760
so it's a Pyrrhic victory in either case.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.950
- [Barry] Quiet please,
actual work going on here.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:11.500
- Is that that same bee?

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.140
- [Barry] Yes, it is.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.305
- I'm helping him sue the human race.

00:02:15.305 --> 00:02:16.240
- What?

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.090
- Okay, so not only can
an individual not sue

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.140
the entire human race because
that lawsuit would fail,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.280
a florist cannot help you
sue another party in court.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.790
That is called the
unauthorized practice of law.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.820
So, she can actually
get herself into trouble

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.370
for practicing law when she
doesn't have a law license.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.930
- Maybe this could make
up for it a little bit.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.510
You know, Barry, this
lawsuit's a pretty big deal.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:41.343
- [Barry] I guess.

00:02:41.343 --> 00:02:43.350
- Are you sure that you
wanna go through with it?

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.490
- Am I sure?

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.280
When I'm done with the humans,
they won't be able to say

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.620
honey, I'm home without paying a royalty.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.130
(dramatic music)

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:53.630
- Okay, well, one of the
reasons you hire an attorney

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.370
is so that you can file your complaint

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.710
that starts the lawsuit in a
way that the court will accept.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:02.030
You cannot mail in your
complaint to most courts.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.420
You have to actually go
down to the courthouse,

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.450
file the complaint itself,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.850
and serve that complaint on other people.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.930
Here, what Vanessa has done
is she's just put a document

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.230
into the mail, mailed it to the court,

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.390
and nothing is going to happen.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.040
They will probably just throw that out

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.180
because that's not the
proper way to start a lawsuit

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:21.200
in New York.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.850
Also, if we can rewind it,

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:24.970
we can see that she's also
addressed the complaint

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:26.610
to the wrong place.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.510
She has addressed her complaint

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.540
to the Superior Court of New York.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:34.530
Now, while the Superior
Court is the correct court

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:38.160
in California, the trial court in New York

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.190
is actually called the Supreme Court.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.640
I think the writers for the Bee Movie

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.520
probably work in Los
Angeles or California,

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.517
where you call the trial
court the Superior Court.

00:03:47.517 --> 00:03:49.320
That's not the case in New York,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.000
so there are many, many reasons why

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.470
this complaint would be rejected
by the court in New York.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:59.960
- All right, case number 4475,
Superior Court of New York,

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.700
Barry Bee Benson versus the honey industry

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:03.883
is now in session.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.640
Mr. Montgomery, you're representing

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.480
the five major food
companies collectively.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.590
- A privilege.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:11.590
- Okay, so this is interesting.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.240
They actually rolled back the lawsuit

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.640
when they got to court itself.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.240
Instead of suing humanity,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.950
they are only suing five
farmers who happen to keep bees

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.840
and happen to harvest and sell the honey.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.530
This lawsuit also doesn't work,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.483
but for slightly different reasons.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:32.450
- Mr. Benson, you're representing
all bees of the world?

00:04:32.945 --> 00:04:35.650
(clears throat)
(suspenseful music)

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.181
- [Audience] Huh?

00:04:38.181 --> 00:04:40.848
(Barry buzzing)

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:44.640
- I'm kidding, I'm kidding.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.730
Yes, Your Honor, we're ready to proceed.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.390
- All right, so, what Barry
Benson is trying to do here

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:51.660
is what's called a class action lawsuit,

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.580
where a few representative plaintiffs

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.030
claim that they are representative

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:58.920
of a larger class of
people, and as a result,

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.670
they claim that they should be
able to prosecute the lawsuit

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:05.350
on behalf of all of those people
who are similarly affected.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:09.230
So, would Barry Benson be
able to proceed further

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:10.960
with a class action lawsuit?

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:12.881
Well, let's think like a lawyer.

00:05:12.881 --> 00:05:16.180
(upbeat rock music)

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.580
Going with the federal definition,

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.380
there are some requirements
you have to meet

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.290
before you're able to proceed further

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:22.540
with a class action lawsuit.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.290
In a federal system, this
is governed by Rule 23

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.260
of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure,

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:28.390
which requires you to have

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.800
one, an adequate class definition.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.420
Two, that the class be ascertainable.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.280
Three, that there must be numerosity,

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.260
in other words, a lot of people.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.090
Four, there must be commonality

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.470
amongst all of the class members.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.510
Five, there must be typicality

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:47.270
between the representative
plaintiffs and the class members.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.740
Six, you have to have adequacy of counsel.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.120
And seven, you have to meet
some other requirements

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.330
to show that class action
status is warranted.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:58.200
Here, all of those
requirements are not met.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.510
Number one, there is a
terrible class definition.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.300
They've defined the class as all bees.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:05.070
Well, some bees haven't
had their honey taken.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.770
Some bees have had their
honey taken in different ways.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.820
So, the class definition
is necessarily problematic.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:13.410
Number two, the class
has to be ascertainable.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.100
There are billions, if not
trillions of bees out there,

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.890
and there is no way to identify

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:19.710
the individually effected bees.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.000
Three, numerosity, there's
a lot of bees out there,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.380
that probably would be met.

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:26.260
But number four is that
there must be commonality

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:27.640
amongst all of the class members,

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.860
and because different bees
are treated differently,

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.850
there's not enough
commonality amongst the class

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:32.683
to move forward.

00:06:32.683 --> 00:06:34.350
Number five, there is no reason to assume

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:35.680
that Barry Benson is typical

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.410
of all of the other class members.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:41.380
Number six, adequacy of counsel
is definitely not there.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:42.670
Barry's not even a lawyer,

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:45.420
so he can't even proceed
with this proceeding,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:49.280
let alone adequately represent
the actual class members

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.980
that he's trying to move forward with.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:51.970
And for the same reasons,

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:53.650
all of the other requirements of Rule 23

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:54.930
are not met as well.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:56.360
So, basically, this class action

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:58.500
is absolutely doomed to fail

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.886
and it would not move forward
in any way whatsoever.

00:07:01.886 --> 00:07:03.340
(sighing)

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.970
- And Mr. Montgomery, your
opening statement, please.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.070
- Opening statement?

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.060
You don't go straight
from the first hearing

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:12.470
and filing a complaint right into trial.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:14.000
That's totally ridiculous.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.350
There would be years of pretrial discovery

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.330
and motion practice before
you even got to trial,

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:20.520
let alone trial happening
on the first day.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.220
- Ladies and gentlemen of the jury,

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:26.550
my grandmother was a simple woman.

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:27.810
- Why is there a jury in this case?

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:29.240
That also doesn't make sense.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.440
And I've mentioned this
a million times before,

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.070
you don't get to go
straight from counsel table,

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:35.800
walk right into the well of the court,

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.160
and then go straight at the jury.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:39.740
Judges and juries really hate it

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:42.500
when you invade the
personal space of the jury,

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.210
and if you are aggressive about it,

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:46.012
the bailiff will tackle you.

00:07:46.012 --> 00:07:48.200
- Talking bee.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.480
How do we know this isn't some sort of

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:54.410
holographic motion picture
capture Hollywood wizardry?

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.737
They could be using laser beams.

00:07:56.737 --> 00:07:57.570
(jury gasping)

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.450
Robotics, ventriloquism, cloning.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:03.576
For all we know, he could be on steroids.

00:08:03.576 --> 00:08:05.060
(all gasping)

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:07.440
- That is completely improper argument

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.923
and would be stricken from the record.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:11.893
- [Judge] Mr. Benson.

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:17.460
- Again, he's not a lawyer,

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.463
he doesn't get to give
an opening statement.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:23.270
- Ladies and gentlemen of the jury,

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.450
there's no trickery here.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.330
I'm just an ordinary bee.

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:28.410
- And again, you don't get to fly

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:31.180
straight from counsel
table directly to the ledge

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.860
in front of the jury and then
give your opening statement.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.520
You're invading the
personal space of the jury,

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.930
and both the jury and the judge
are not going to allow that.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:42.440
- And as a bee, honey's
pretty important to me.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:44.200
It's important to all bees.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:45.230
We invented it.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:48.410
We make it and we protect
it with our lives.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:50.730
Unfortunately, there are
some people in this room

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.720
who think they can take
whatever they want from us

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:54.480
because we're the little guys.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.330
And what I'm hoping is that
after this is all over,

00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:59.310
you'll see how by taking our honey,

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.840
you're not only taking
away everything we have,

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:03.740
but everything we are.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.563
- Okay, so, um,

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:11.050
so, bees are what we refer to
in the legal world as chattel.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:12.970
Almost all animals are.

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:15.220
It means that they are animate objects

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:18.360
that are owned by some other human.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.460
And it is fully within the right
of the owner of the chattel

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:24.400
to get the fruits of
those particular animals,

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:26.170
whether it's milk from cows,

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:29.130
or whether it's eggs from
chicken, or honey from bees.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:30.500
If you own those animals,

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.860
generally, you are entitled
to what they produce.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:36.600
But if bees somehow were able to prove

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.410
that they were conscious
and fully sentient,

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.010
then they might have
a conceivable argument

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:46.950
that taking honey from them is
a form of conversion or theft

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:50.820
to which they would be
entitled to some compensation.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.310
Animal rights activists
have tried to give rights

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:56.870
to lots of different animals
in different contexts.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:58.740
For example, the famous selfie picture

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:01.321
that was taken by a particular monkey.

00:10:01.321 --> 00:10:02.950
(playful music)
(monkey squawking)

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:04.580
But until that time,

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:07.240
animals do not have the
same rights as humans.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:08.610
They cannot file a lawsuit,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.160
they cannot defend themselves in court,

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:12.560
and they do not have property rights.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:15.640
- So, Mr. Klauss
Vanderhayden of Honey Farms,

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:17.480
pretty big company you have there.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:18.710
- I suppose so.

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:20.920
- And I see you also own Honeyburton,

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:22.640
and Honron!
(jury gasping)

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:24.620
- Yes, they provide--
- Relevance.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:25.453
- For our farms.

00:10:25.453 --> 00:10:27.990
- Beekeeper, I find that to
be a very disturbing term,

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.020
I have to say.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.020
I don't imagine you employ
any beefreers, do you?

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:34.100
- No.
- I'm sorry--

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.800
- Objection, argumentative.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:36.670
- No.
- No.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:39.620
Because you don't free
bees, you keep bees.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:40.890
- Objection, argumentative.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.610
- It seems you thought a bear
would be an appropriate image

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.370
for a jar of honey.

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:46.990
- Well, they're very lovable creatures.

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:48.310
- Objection, relevance?

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:49.360
Why is this relevant?

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:53.512
- Build-A-Bear?
- Yeah, you mean like this?

00:10:53.512 --> 00:10:56.600
(bear growling)
(people screaming)

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:59.010
Bears kill bees!

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.830
- All right, so, in addition
to being completely irrelevant

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:04.640
to the court proceedings, I
probably don't need to tell you

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.220
that when you bring a
demonstrative into court,

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:09.410
this is a demonstrative, something
that you bring into court

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:13.280
for demonstration of some particular idea,

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:17.250
it cannot be a lethal
animal, it cannot be really,

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:20.550
probably any animal at all
unless it was like a service dog.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:21.920
This is completely improper.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:23.870
This would be thrown out immediately,

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:25.863
and probably a mistrial declared.

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.370
- [Barry] Well, I just
think it was awfully nice

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:30.210
of that bear to pitch in like that.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.080
- I'm telling you, I think
the jury's on our side.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.760
- Are we doing everything
right, you know, legally?

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.100
- I'm a florist.

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:38.560
- Exactly, she's a florist.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:39.960
Why is she sitting at counsel table?

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:41.950
Why is she helping with this lawsuit?

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.093
That is the unauthorized practice of law.

00:11:45.106 --> 00:11:50.106
- You know, I just about had
it with your little mind games.

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:52.163
- What's that?

00:11:52.163 --> 00:11:53.520
- Italian Vogue.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.400
- Mama mia, that's a lot of pages.

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:56.780
- It's a lot of ads.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:58.190
- Remember what Van said.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:00.550
Why is your life any
more valuable than mine?

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:03.101
- That's funny, I just
can't seem to recall that.

00:12:03.101 --> 00:12:04.859
(Barry yelps)

00:12:04.859 --> 00:12:07.560
I think something stinks in here.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:09.010
- So we learned earlier in the movie

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:14.010
that bees cannot be struck by
paper of more than 30 pages.

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:18.500
In a world where bees are
sentient and conscious,

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:21.640
I think attacking a bee
with a magazine of that size

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:24.420
is probably attempted murder.

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:28.631
So, you know, tell that
to all your kids, I guess.

00:12:28.631 --> 00:12:29.890
- Mr. Benson Bee,

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:33.130
I'll ask you what I think
we'd all like to know.

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:37.603
What exactly is your
relationship to that woman?

00:12:38.452 --> 00:12:39.285
(Vanessa gasps)

00:12:39.285 --> 00:12:40.870
- Relevance, character?

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:42.310
- Good friends?
- Yes.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:43.370
- How good?

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:46.900
Do you live together?
- Wait a minute,

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.797
this isn't about--
- Are you her little

00:12:48.797 --> 00:12:51.086
(clears throat)
bedbug?

00:12:51.086 --> 00:12:52.870
- Hey, that's not the kind--
- I've seen a bee documentary.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:56.070
- People and bees should be
making objections right now.

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:58.890
- Queen give birth to all the
bee children in the hives?

00:12:58.890 --> 00:12:59.723
- Yeah, but--

00:12:59.723 --> 00:13:02.031
- So those aren't even your real parents.

00:13:02.031 --> 00:13:03.840
- Oh Barry.
- Yes, they are.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.010
- Improper, character, relevance.

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:08.560
- An illegitimate bee, aren't you, Benson?

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:09.740
- He's denouncing bees.

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:12.710
- And don't y'all date your cousins?

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:14.262
- Objection.
- I'm gonna pincushion--

00:13:14.262 --> 00:13:16.934
- Ah, the non-lawyer makes an objection.

00:13:16.934 --> 00:13:18.780
(Barry gasps)
(dramatic music)

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:20.480
- Adam, don't, it's what he wants.

00:13:23.982 --> 00:13:25.448
(Adam groans)

00:13:25.448 --> 00:13:27.257
- Oh, I'm hit!

00:13:27.257 --> 00:13:29.690
- Didn't that bee just commit suicide?

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:30.750
Important to note that

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:33.890
one of the reasons we have a
legal system in the first place

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:36.390
is so that we don't
have to rely on violence

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:37.780
to solve our problems.

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:39.930
And the bees here are
supposed to be the good guys.

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:42.930
They're trying to get just
outcome from their lawsuit,

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:46.190
and yet, the bee co-counsel
here has given in

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:49.700
to his aggression and he has
attacked the opposing party,

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:54.178
the opposing attorney,
with his venomous stinger.

00:13:54.178 --> 00:13:56.450
That's not something that you would want

00:13:56.450 --> 00:13:57.720
an officer of the court to do,

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.330
and if in fact he was a real lawyer,

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.220
he'd probably get disbarred for that.

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:05.230
- And assuming you've
done step 29 correctly,

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:06.593
you're ready for the tub.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:11.220
- Yeah, you don't get to
talk to the jury like that

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:12.560
without the presence of the judge.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:14.150
- Mr. Flayman.

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:15.890
- Yes, yes, yes, Your Honor.

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:17.930
- [Judge] Where is the rest of your team?

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:20.880
- Uh, well, Your Honor, it's interesting.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:23.550
You know, bees are trained
to fly kind of haphazardly,

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:27.060
and as a result, quite often,
we don't make very good time.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.790
- So, I should point out,
if you are not in court

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.530
when the judge tells you to be in court,

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:33.730
you're in a world of hurt.

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:36.810
Judges hate it when lawyers
don't show up on time.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:39.360
They can issue sanctions,
and in the worst case,

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.630
they can dismiss your case
for not showing up on time.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:43.810
You know, and on further inspection,

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:47.650
that co-counsel bee has
attacked the opposing counsel.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:49.630
There is absolutely no way

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:51.990
that he would be allowed to
sit at counsel table anymore.

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:53.490
Number one, he would be arrested.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.730
Number two, the judge would
probably have to declare

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:59.260
a mistrial because of the
outburst that occurred.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:02.040
It's not fair to either
party to have to proceed

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:06.260
with a tainted jury as a result
of the lawyer's bad conduct

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:09.120
in assaulting each other.

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:11.340
- How much longer are we going to allow

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:13.980
these absurd shenanigans to go on?

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:16.890
They have presented no compelling evidence

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:19.500
to support their charges
against my clients,

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:23.290
who all run perfectly
legitimate businesses.

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:26.910
I move for a complete
dismissal of this entire case.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:28.100
- So this happens a lot in movies,

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:29.610
where the opposing side will say

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:31.700
that the other side has
presented no evidence whatsoever

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:32.800
and the case should be thrown out.

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.300
And usually when they say that,

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:35.750
it's at the beginning of the case,

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:38.370
when there wouldn't
actually be any evidence.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:41.190
Here, the plaintiff bees
have had ample opportunity

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.380
to demonstrate the validity of their case

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:45.930
by presenting evidence,
and they simply haven't.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:47.790
They've engaged in a bunch of shenanigans,

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:50.420
they have not presented
any relevant evidence,

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:53.760
and as a result, the opposing
side here is making a motion

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:54.930
to dismiss the case.

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:58.940
That is actually something you
can do in a real court case.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:02.750
You can move for what's called
a directed verdict or a JNOV.

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:04.200
It basically just means that

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:06.020
the plaintiff hasn't met their burden

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:07.570
and there's no reason in proceeding

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:09.010
into the defendant's case

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:11.420
because the plaintiff hasn't
met their evidentiary burden

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:12.600
in the first place.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:14.250
So this is the first accurate thing

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:15.460
that this movie has done.

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:19.130
This is the time to make a
motion for a directed verdict.

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:20.640
- Where's your proof?

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.070
Where's the evidence?

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:24.293
Show me the smoking gun.

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:26.990
- Hold it, Your Honor.

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:28.580
You want a smoking gun?

00:16:28.580 --> 00:16:32.052
Here is your smoking gun.
(dramatic music)

00:16:32.052 --> 00:16:34.100
(jury gasping)

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:35.350
- What is that?

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:36.960
- It's a bee smoker.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:37.853
- What, this?

00:16:37.853 --> 00:16:40.430
This harmless little contraption?

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:43.161
This couldn't hurt a fly, let alone a bee.

00:16:43.161 --> 00:16:45.630
(smoker hissing)
(bees clamoring)

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:47.590
- So this is somewhat relevant evidence.

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:48.640
You would use the smoke gun

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:50.860
to demonstrate that the
bees haven't consented

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:53.290
to having their honey
taken away from them,

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:55.640
which is one of the
elements you have to show

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:58.550
for theft or conversion.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.210
That being said, there are
so many other hurdles here,

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:04.200
that this would be just a
tiny bit of relevant evidence.

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:06.810
It really doesn't get to
the end of the court case,

00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:09.940
but still, at least it's
relevant evidence for once.

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:14.022
- Ladies and gentlemen,
please free these bees.

00:17:14.022 --> 00:17:14.855
(all cheering)

00:17:14.855 --> 00:17:17.589
- Free the bees, free the bees!

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:20.006
Free the bees, free the bees!

00:17:20.981 --> 00:17:22.210
Free the bees!

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:25.363
- The court finds in favor of the bees.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:28.570
- Yeah, that's something
that happens in movies

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:31.400
all the time, it does not
happen in real life at all.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:34.110
The judge cannot decide in
the middle of the court case

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:37.140
to just grant the verdict
in favor of the plaintiffs.

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.560
This would be overturned
on appeal immediately

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:43.490
because the defendants have
been denied their due process.

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:45.250
- [Reporter] Congratulations
on your victory.

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:47.500
What are you going to
demand as a settlement?

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:49.280
- [Barry] First, we're gonna
demand a complete shutdown

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:50.783
of all bee work camps.

00:17:50.783 --> 00:17:52.283
- Yeah, that would not happen.

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:54.870
- [Barry] Then we wanna
get back all the honey

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:58.270
that was ours to begin
with, every last drop.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:00.300
- So, when you win a lawsuit,

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:04.500
generally you are entitled
to monetary compensation.

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:07.970
You can sometimes get back
the actual physical things

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:09.650
that you feel you are entitled to.

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:12.210
That is a legal concept
called specific performance.

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:14.770
You are getting the defendants to give you

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:18.380
the specific thing that you
think you're entitled to.

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:20.820
Generally, that happens
in real estate cases

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:24.220
because real estate is always unique,

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:28.610
but if it's possible to
give financial compensation,

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.560
the court almost always will
give financial compensation.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:32.740
And there's no reason

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:35.370
that the bees couldn't be
given financial compensation

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.680
instead of a return of all of the honey.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.150
On top of that, all of the
things that they are getting

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:43.160
in this potential settlement

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:45.070
would never be given by the court,

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:48.740
it's completely unrealistic
and would never happen.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:52.010
- We demand an end to the
glorification of the bear

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:54.210
as anything more than a filthy, smelly,

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:56.210
bigheaded, bad-breath, stink machine.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:57.976
I believe we're all aware of
what they do in the woods.

00:18:57.976 --> 00:18:59.870
(grunting)

00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:01.253
Wait for my signal.

00:19:02.915 --> 00:19:03.748
(gun cocks)
- Huh?

00:19:03.748 --> 00:19:04.581
- [Barry] Take him out.

00:19:04.581 --> 00:19:06.640
(gun fires)
(Pooh yelps)

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.560
- He'll have a little
nausea for a few hours,

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:09.660
then he'll be fine.

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:13.540
- Yeah, if you're entitled to
compensation for your honey,

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:16.340
you don't then get to assault other people

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:17.870
for some weird reason.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:20.010
Why the bees hate the bears?

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:22.140
That's a whole separate issue, I guess.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:23.610
- [Barry] And we will no longer tolerate

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:25.930
derogatory bee-negative nicknames.

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:28.200
- But it's just a prance-about stage name.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:30.200
- And that's a First Amendment violation.

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:31.360
- [Barry] Health products

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:34.683
and la-dee-da human tea
time snack garnishments.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:37.150
(groaning)

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:39.760
- And there's no reason why
an injunction would be granted

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:42.130
to prevent people from using the honey

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:44.052
that's already in the stream of commerce.

00:19:44.052 --> 00:19:46.719
(playful music)

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:52.060
Okay, so that's the Bee Movie,
but how realistic is it?

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:54.140
Well, it's time to give
the Bee Movie a grade

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:55.752
for legal realism.

00:19:55.752 --> 00:19:58.610
(gavel banging)

00:19:58.610 --> 00:20:01.030
The Bee Movie effectively
gets everything wrong

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:02.470
about lawsuits.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:05.030
Bees and florists cannot be lawyers.

00:20:05.030 --> 00:20:08.240
The process by which the
court proceeded in this case

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:11.740
is completely wrong and
would be grounds for appeal.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:13.100
Everything that happened in that case

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:14.510
is completely unrealistic.

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:15.700
About the only thing they got right

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.670
was that the opposing
attorney made a motion

00:20:18.670 --> 00:20:21.510
for a directed verdict
at about the right time.

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:26.510
So, I give the Bee Movie a
solid B for legal realism.

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:28.500
Actually, I'm just kidding, it gets an F.

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:29.333
It's terrible.

00:20:29.333 --> 00:20:31.840
It's the most unrealistic thing
I've ever seen in my life.

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.610
But, you know, kids movie,
so what are you gonna do?

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:37.880
- If you don't move, he
won't sting you, freeze.

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:39.030
- The Bee Movie teaches us

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:41.020
that it's okay to be
curious about your world.

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:43.435
It also teaches us that it's
hard out there for a bee.

00:20:43.435 --> 00:20:45.757
(Barry yelping)

00:20:45.757 --> 00:20:46.590
- Help me!

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:49.460
- Even though bees can't sue humanity,

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:51.930
they are facing a crisis of extinction.

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:53.090
After watching the Bee Movie,

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:54.960
I pulled up a great documentary about bees

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:57.350
on CuriosityStream called More Than Honey.

00:20:57.350 --> 00:20:59.590
It talks about this
worldwide bee phenomenon,

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:02.478
why bees are in danger and
what we can do about it.

00:21:02.478 --> 00:21:04.990
- I'm just saying all life has value.

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:07.370
You don't know what
he's capable of feeling.

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:10.200
- Real bees are not as
cute as the Bee Movie,

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:12.910
but they still play a vital
role in our ecosystem.

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:15.590
If you're as curious about
the world as Barry Bee Benson,

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:16.910
you should check out CuriosityStream.

